// Practice quiz: Logging
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/exceptions-and-logging/logging

::o1 levels and config::What is the main reason to use a logger instead of System.out.println? {
~A logger is always faster than println#Not quite. Speed is not the reason.
~A logger removes secrets from messages automatically#Not quite. A logger writes whatever it is given.
~println cannot write to the console#Not quite. println writes to the console; it just has no levels.
=Each message has a level, and configuration chooses which levels are written and where, without changing code#Correct. That control is what println cannot give you.
}

::o1 destination::Where does a logger's output go? {
~Always to the console#Not quite. The console is only one possible destination.
~Always to a file next to the program#Not quite. A file is one option, chosen by configuration.
~Only to System.out#Not quite. For example, slf4j-simple writes to System.err by default, and other back ends can write elsewhere.
=Wherever the logging configuration sends it, such as the console or a file#Correct. The destination is configuration, not code.
}

::o2 handled problem::A nightly import skipped 3 malformed rows and finished the rest. Which level fits best? {
~ERROR#Not quite. The import did not fail; it handled the bad rows.
=WARN#Correct. Something unexpected happened, but the program handled it.
~DEBUG#Not quite. Someone should be able to see this when DEBUG is off.
~INFO#Not quite. Skipped data is not a normal milestone.
}

::o2 threshold::The log threshold is set to WARN. Which messages are written? {
~Only WARN#Not quite. ERROR is above WARN, so it is written too.
~DEBUG, INFO and WARN#Not quite. Levels below the threshold are dropped, not kept.
=WARN and ERROR#Correct. A threshold writes its own level and every level above it.
~Only ERROR#Not quite. WARN is at the threshold, so it is written.
}

::o3 trace argument::In an SLF4J call, where must the exception go for its stack trace to be printed? {
=As the last argument, after the arguments that fill the placeholders#Correct. SLF4J treats a final Throwable argument as the exception and prints its stack trace.
~As the first argument#Not quite. The first argument is the message.
~Inside the message, using e.getMessage()#Not quite. That prints only the message text, not the stack trace.
~Anywhere, because an error call always prints a trace#Not quite. The level does not add a trace; the exception argument does.
}

::o3 safe detail::A login failed. Which detail is safe to put in the log line? {
~The password that was typed#Not quite. Passwords must never be logged, even wrong ones.
=The user id#Correct. An id identifies the account without exposing anything secret.
~The session token#Not quite. Anyone who reads the log could reuse the token.
~The user's full card number on file#Not quite. Card numbers are sensitive and must stay out of logs.
}
